Q: Abstract Api Validation for Mobile number A: <p>The reason your code is failing is due to two common PHP issues:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Missing <code>CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER</code>:</strong> Without this flag, <code>curl_exec()</code> outputs the API response directly to the screen and assigns a boolean <code>true</code> to your <code>$response</code> variable. That ruins your JSON decoding.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Casting an Array to a String:</strong> <code>json_decode($response, true)</code> creates an associative array. When you use <code>$check = (string)$data;</code>, PHP converts the array to the literal string <code>&quot;Array&quot;</code>. Using <code>strpos</code> to search for <code>'true'</code> inside the word <code>&quot;Array&quot;</code> will always fail.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is the corrected PHP logic:<br />
PHP</p>
<pre><code>$ch = curl_init(); 
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_URL, 'https://phonevalidation.abstractapi.com/v1/?api_key=my_api&amp;phone=14152007986'); 
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true); // Ensures the response string is saved to the variable
$response = curl_exec($ch); 
curl_close($ch); 

$data = json_decode($response, true);  
// Directly check the ‘valid’ boolean key in the decoded array if (isset($data\[‘valid’\]) &amp;&amp; $data\[‘valid’\] === true) { echo “PhoneNo format is valid”; } else { echo “PhoneNo format is invalid”; }

</code></pre>
